erified: remote.cljc:29, 981, 1023, 1038, 1046) | remote.md 8 |\n    23\t| GLM MUST 2 bounded meeting work | FIXED. Board retention, active pairs, per-step fanout are composition bounds; exhaustion is refused by a gate and observed as `:dao.stream/refused`; the NAT-binding keep-alive cadence sentence restored | remote.md 4 |\n    24\t| GLM SHOULD 1-6, 8, 10-12 | FIXED (1 = gate MUST 7; 2 dao.jing.md \"today\"+target; 3 accept-bytes! marked target; 4 btree row annotated; 5 slice 5 names rpc's fate; 6 driver consumers corrected to dao.space-via-coordinate and yin.repl.link, yin.vm.content qualified; 8 cut list applied; 10 key-hash name form only; 11 third-party renewal sentence; 12 one namespace spelling in plan 1) | as listed |\n    25\t| GLM SHOULD 7 stale-forward docs | PARTIAL. Pointers added in dao.jing.hash-registry.md and dao.jing.call-site-classification.md (both under docs/design/); docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md is outside docs/design/ and outside my ownership: not edited, listed below | two files |\n    26\t| GLM SHOULD 9 terminology | PARTIAL. UCF \"facade\" leftovers renamed to reflection (3 spots) and the acceptance row now cites the plan's slice 8; ws.md's residual server/client wording deferred: it tracks dao.stream.md's own Close-section wording, so re-wording it ahead of the code slices would desync transport spec from contract; recorded in plan 3 as a with-the-code item | UCF; plan 3 |\n    27\t\n    28\t## 2. Design choices (rulings and owner statements touched)\n    29\t\n    30\t- **Piggyback dropped, not routed** (MUST-FIX 3, my call per the brief): routing it would run a `cursor` per answer through middleware on the serving peer, a cost paid on every request to buy one saved round trip at attach; dropping is strictly simpler and fixes the surface bypass. Cost stated in the spec.\n    31\t- **Protocol errors are a key on the answer, not a new frame** (MUST-FIX 1): the no-new-frames ruling holds; `channel-gone` is a reason value on the reflection's outcome, not a wire shape.\n    32\t- **Surface in the descriptor answer** (MUST-FIX 1): the gate allowed descriptor answer or descriptor field; the answer key keeps the descriptor itself reachability-only, as 2.2 already ruled.\n    33\t- **OWNER-VISIBLE, encryption**: remote channel confidentiality is deferred, not specified. A relay peer (pair channel, meeting peer) reads every request and answer envelope in the clear regardless of value-level middleware. Owner statement 6 is met for value middleware only.\n    34\t- **OWNER-VISIBLE, gate/ShiBi**: the seam hosts a ShiBi built from index and query interpreters, with one stated limitation: the gate observes the index's latest published decision per medium, not the history it folded. Owner statement 8 is satisfied (the fold lives in an interpreter); the \"four prohibitions\" are amended as above, still within datom.world.md's six invariants (state is wrap-held, no callbacks, no hidden or shared global state, explicit chains).\n    35\t- **OWNER-VISIBLE, length**: 675 against the under-600 target; arithmetic above. If the owner wants the number, the honest lever is relaxing \"do not drop a rule\", not further compression.\n    36\t- Impossibility ruling kept and sharpened: both-unreachable peers need a third reachable peer; peers with no outbound path to a reachable WebSocket peer or relay cannot communicate at all.\n    37\t\n    38\t## 3. Files\n    39\t\n    40\tCreated: `docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md` (162). Rewritten: `dao.stream.remote.md` (725 to 675), `dao.stream.middleware.md` (153 to 169), `dao.shibi.md` (45 to 47). Edited: `dao.stream.md` (1003), `dao.jing.cbor.md` (691), `dao.jing.md` (629), `dao.lease.md` (304), `dao.data.btree.md` (1305), `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (1840), `yin.vm.linker.md` (2790), `dao.jing.hash-registry.md` (686), `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md` (273). The last seven are the \"now false because of what I changed\" set: framing sentences, the lease carriage word, the btree target row, UCF's retired term and moved slice citation, the linker vocabulary citation now pointing at plan section 1, and two stale-forward inventories.\n    41\t\n    42\t## 4. New contradictions found, not fixed\n    43\t\n    44\t- `docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md:241-246` still instructs `default-handlers`/`connect-content!` (outside docs/design/, outside ownership).\n    45\t- `dao.stream.ws.md` keeps server/client wording in its mechanics sections, tracking `dao.stream.md` Close's own \"server-side accepted-connection handle\"; deferred to the code slices (plan 3).\n    46\t- Task-header bookkeeping (GLM OTHER): the stub is `docs/design/dao.shibi.md`, not `shibi.md`.\n    47\t\n    48\t## 5. Mechanical checks\n    49\t\n    50\tRan: non-ASCII scan (clean on new/rewritten files); em-dash scan (none introduced; pre-existing ones in other docs preserved verbatim); 170-column check (clean on my output; dao.stream.md/dao.jing.md long lines are pre-existing tables); server/client scan of the spec (owner quotes, the quoted \"server\" convention, and \"stepped client\"/\"content client\" module names only); piggyback and amendment-count greps; table-border closure on the plan's slice table; collab-reference scan; cross-reference sweep proving no design doc still cites the spec's moved sections 9/10 (UCF was the one, fixed). Verified against the tree before fixing: serving's second consumer, the coordinate opener at query.cljc:250, yin.vm/content.cljc's absence of a remote path, DHT's Transit-per-datagram, discovery.md's key-hash-only name form, the old serve draft's meeting bounds and NAT-lifetime wording. Not verified: no build or test run (per the brief); box-table column alignment not tool-checked (awk denied; by construction); pre-existing citations inside docs I did not fully open were not re-verified.\n    51\t\n    52\t## 6. Ready for re-gate?\n    53\t\n    54\tYes. The two known soft spots a gate should press: the 675-line length against the under-600 target (arithmetic above), and the gate's decision-read contract in middleware.md, which is the newest machinery in this round.\n     1\t# DaoStream Remote: Any Stream, Reachable As Itself\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement. Every sentence below is a rule. It replaces the earlier serve\n     6\tdraft (git history, `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md` at `71f3fb93`) and\n     7\tsucceeds `dao.jing.remote`, which is deprecated whole once this is\n     8\timplemented (section 8). Implementation order, slices and the\n     9\tcompanion-edit inventory live in\n    10\t[`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md).\n    11\t\n    12\t## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n    13\t\n    14\tThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n    15\t\n    16\t> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n    17\t> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n    18\t> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n    19\t> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n    20\t> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n    21\t\n    22\t> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n    23\t> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n    24\t> client.\n    25\t\n    26\t`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\n    27\tdocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\n    28\tits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\n    29\tcontract beyond the amendments section 9 lists (the three accepted decisions\n    30\tOD-1, OD-2 and OD-3, the `:dao.stream/refused` row, and the composed-handle\n    31\tsentence).\n    32\t\n    33\t**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n    34\t\n    35\t- Direct exchange needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n    36\t  coordinated simultaneous send. Where neither exists, a third peer with a\n    37\t  reachable address is required (section 4).\n    38\t- The unreachable cases are named by mapping and filtering behavior, not\n    39\t  product name: endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a\n    40\t  symmetric NAT; the strict paths of a carrier-grade NAT, which is address\n    41\t  sharing and not one behavior, since other carrier-grade paths hole-punch)\n    42\t  defeats punching, and relay is the workaround (section 4).\n    43\t- A browser cannot listen. It dials, or it is reached through a third peer.\n    44\t- Every peer needs an outbound path to a reachable WebSocket peer or relay.\n    45\t  If UDP is blocked and no such path exists, communication is impossible.\n    46\t- Everything a channel carries is readable by every peer that carries or\n    47\t  receives it until encryption is composed, and the encryption this version\n    48\t  specifies is value-level only: a relay still sees every envelope,\n    49\t  identities, ops, cursors, ids, sizes and timing included (section 7).\n    50\t- UDP messages are bounded by a composed maximum (section 3.2).\n    51\t- A reclaimed lease means the resource is gone, not disconnected (section 6).\n    52\t- A false lapse under partition is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n    53\t\n    54\t**Non-goals.** Authentication, authorization and key distribution (section 7\n    55\tnames the seam; `dao.shibi.md` owns the rest); channel-level encryption\n    56\t(deferred, section 7); WebRTC data channels; browser inbound; readiness\n    57\tnotification; exactly-once delivery.\n    58\t\n    59\t## 2. The design: mirror and reflection\n    60\t\n    61\tOne sentence: a peer that holds a handle answers the handle's own operations\n    62\tas data over a channel; a peer at the other end holds a handle whose\n    63\toperations are those questions asked across the channel, with the source's\n    64\toutcome maps returned verbatim. Four concepts.\n    65\t\n    66\t- **channel**: two ordinary handles with exactly two ends, a writer whose\n    67\t  `append!` carries one value toward the other end and a reader positioned\n    68\t  on the values the other end sent; built from a WebSocket attachment and\n    69\t  its projection (3.1), a UDP socket adapter and its deposit medium (3.2),\n    70\t  two ring buffers in one process, or two reflections served by a third\n    71\t  peer (3.3).\n    72\t- **table**: composition data on a peer, `{identity {:handle h :surface\n    73\t  S}}` plus the optional keys of sections 6 and 7. A plain map the\n    74\t  composition owns, not a registry (`dao.stream.md`, No hidden global\n    75\t  state).\n    76\t- **mirror step**: the pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer\n    77\t  requests against its table. It holds no state between calls beyond the\n    78\t  channel reader's cursor.\n    79\t- **reflection**: the handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A\n    80\t  **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections on that channel\n    81\t  share.\n    82\t\n    83\tEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\n    84\treflections, on the same channel, in both directions. No request names a\n    85\tkind of peer; the protocol has no peer identity at all. A peer is a channel\n    86\tend.\n    87\t\n    88\t### 2.1 Wire shapes\n    89\t\n    90\tTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n    91\t`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\n    92\tthe transport-owned namespace rule of `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes).\n    93\t\n    94\t```clojure\n    95\t;; request\n    96\t{:dao.stream/identity        id\n    97\t :dao.stream.remote/op       op        ; :dao.stream/descriptor | :dao.stream/cursor\n    98\t                                       ; | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    99\t :dao.stream.remote/args     [...]     ; []  | [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n   100\t :dao.stream.remote/id       n         ; asker-minted, unique per channel reader the asker holds\n   101\t :dao.stream.remote/budget   k}        ; optional, next only, a positive integer\n   102\t\n   103\t;; answer, the ordinary case: the contract's outcome map for the requested\n   104\t;; operation, verbatim, plus\n   105\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   106\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   107\t :dao.stream.remote/surface  S         ; descriptor answers only: the entry's declared surface\n   108\t :dao.stream.remote/more     [...]}    ; optional, next only: further outcome maps in order\n   109\t\n   110\t;; answer, a protocol error: one shape for every operation, a key instead of\n   111\t;; an outcome map, because no operation's outcome set can carry it\n   112\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   113\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   114\t :dao.stream.remote/error    e}        ; one of the three errors below\n   115\t```\n   116\t\n   117\tA request is one value on the channel writer; an answer is one value on the\n   118\tother end's. The request map is open: keys not named here pass to the mirror\n   119\tstep's middleware chain (section 7) and are otherwise ignored. A value that\n   120\tis neither a well-formed request nor a well-formed answer is dropped as a\n   121\tdiagnostic below the mirror step and the link, as malformed wire input\n   122\t(`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries). Wire ops are exactly four: `close!`\n   123\tnever crosses, because the mirror holds nothing per remote party to close\n   124\tand the owner's close is never remote; `attach!` never crosses, because it\n   125\tconsumes a descriptor and produces a local handle.\n   126\t\n   127\t**Protocol errors.** The mirror answers three errors with the error key,\n   128\tnever by fabricating an outcome the operation's closed set forbids\n   129\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention; Surfaces):\n   130\t\n   131\t- `not-found`: the identity is absent from the table, whether never served,\n   132\t  retired by policy, or reclaimed with its lease. An identity this peer does\n   133\t  not serve has no operations and no surface, so this one error is the\n   134\t  honest answer for all four ops.\n   135\t- `no-surface`: the op is outside the entry's declared surface, under the\n   136\t  fixed mapping: `cursor` and `next` need `:reader`, `append!` needs\n   137\t  `:writer`, `descriptor` needs no surface and is always answerable.\n   138\t- `oversize`: the channel writer refused the answer value itself\n   139\t  (`invalid-value`); the read is not skipped and no cursor advances past\n   140\t  it. Large media travel as `dao.jing` addresses (`dao.stream.md`,\n   141\t  Granularity).\n   142\t\n   143\tAll three names live under `:dao.stream.remote/`. The declared surface is\n   144\tanswer data, not descriptor data: the remote descriptor (2.2) carries no\n   145\tsurface, and the `descriptor` answer carries it under\n   146\t`:dao.stream.remote/surface`, the only place the surface is learned.\n   147\t\n   148\t### 2.2 The remote descriptor\n   149\t\n   150\t```clojure\n   151\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/remote\n   152\t :dao.stream/identity  id                      ; the served logical stream\n   153\t :dao.stream/channel   <channel descriptor>}   ; section 3: ws, udp or pair\n   154\t```\n   155\t\n   156\tThe descriptor names a stream and a channel. It carries no authorization, no\n   157\tsurface and no anchors; the mirror answers those. Two remote descriptors\n   158\tthat name one identity through different channels reach one stream, as the\n   159\tcontract's Envelopes section requires of descriptors for one logical stream\n   160\tserved by one transport (OD-3 (a), accepted). Attaching through this\n   161\ttransport never creates: a dynamic dispatch table has an\n   162\t`:dao.stream/attach` entry for `:dao.stream/remote` and no\n   163\t`:dao.stream/create` entry.\n   164\t\n   165\t### 2.3 The mirror step\n   166\t\n   167\t`(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) -> cursor'`. For each\n   168\trequest read from `chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n   169\t\n   170\t1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append the answer\n   171\t   with `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/not-found`.\n   172\t2. Present, but the op outside the entry's declared surface under 2.1's\n   173\t   mapping: append the answer with\n   174\t   `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/no-surface`.\n   175\t3. Otherwise answer `:dao.stream/descriptor` from the entry's handle\n   176\t   directly, adding `:dao.stream.remote/surface`; and apply\n   177\t   `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and `:dao.stream/append!` to it\n   178\t   through `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request`\n   179\t   (`dao.stream.middleware.md`), with context\n   180\t   `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <channel attachment identity>}`. The budget\n   181\t   chase below runs the same `next` through the same path.\n   182\t4. Append the answer with `:dao.stream.remote/id` and\n   183\t   `:dao.stream/identity`.\n   184\t\n   185\tFor `:dao.stream/next` with a budget `k` the mirror may follow the successor\n   186\tcursor of each `ok` outcome up to `k - 1` further times and place the\n   187\tfurther outcome maps, in order, under `:dao.stream.remote/more`. The first\n   188\toutcome is the answer map itself. The vector ends at and includes the first\n   189\tnon-`ok` outcome when one occurs, so `blocked`, `end` and `gap` reach the\n   190\treader with the source's own recovery cursor. A mirror that ignores the\n   191\tbudget is correct. A value the channel cannot carry is answered with\n   192\t`:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/oversize` in place of that\n   193\telement (2.1).\n   194\t\n   195\tSteps 1 to 4 are the mirror's only contacts with the entry's handle: no\n   196\tanswer path calls a handle outside `apply-request`, so nothing bypasses the\n   197\tentry's middleware or its declared surface. The cost is deliberate: a\n   198\treflection mints every anchor, `:oldest` and `:newest` included, by\n   199\trequest, where an earlier draft piggybacked the source's current bounds on\n   200\tevery answer.\n   201\t\n   202\tThe mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or an\n   203\toutcome. The served stream is the original: its cursors, its positions, its\n   204\t`gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\n   205\trests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\n   206\taccepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\n   207\ta composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n   208\t\n   209\t### 2.4 The reflection\n   210\t\n   211\t`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n   212\treflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n   213\tcontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n   214\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   215\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   216\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   217\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   218\tkeyed by id; an optional event writer; and the policy datum\n   219\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n   220\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '209,410p;410,565p;565,675p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '1,70p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '510,560p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '25,42p;425,442p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '209,410p;410,565p;565,675p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '1,70p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '510,560p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md | sed -n '25,42p;425,442p'\"","aggregated_output":"   209\t### 2.4 The reflection\n   210\t\n   211\t`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n   212\treflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n   213\tcontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n   214\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   215\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   216\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   217\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   218\tkeyed by id; an optional event writer; and the policy datum\n   219\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n   220\t\n   221\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   222\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. The `not-found` error marks the\n   223\treflection **gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and declared\n   224\tsurface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   225\t\n   226\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   227\treader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\n   228\teach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   229\tfiling each protocol error under its id. Answers whose id is not\n   230\toutstanding are dropped. Non-answer values are dropped as diagnostics.\n   231\tThis is where the asynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony\n   232\tgoes): the caller's own polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n   233\t\n   234\tThen the operation answers as follows.\n   235\t\n   236\t- `descriptor`: local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with\n   237\t  and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as the source's own descriptor,\n   238\t  different reachability.\n   239\t- `cursor` `a`: a filed answer for `a` is returned, and a filed protocol\n   240\t  error is translated per the rules below. Otherwise a `cursor` request for\n   241\t  `a` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is `transport-error`\n   242\t  with `:dao.stream/retry? true`: the source's map, `invalid-anchor`\n   243\t  included, arrives later as an answer.\n   244\t- `next` `c`: a filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a\n   245\t  `next` request for `c` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is\n   246\t  `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle\n   247\t  yet.\n   248\t- `append!` `v`: send the request and return the channel writer's outcome.\n   249\t  `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly `dao.stream.md`\n   250\t  Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream\n   251\t  elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n   252\t  is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n   253\t- `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n   254\t  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n   255\t  filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n   256\t\n   257\t**Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n   258\treflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n   259\tanswers `transport-error` with\n   260\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n   261\t`descriptor` still answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A filed\n   262\t`no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n   263\toperation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n   264\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n   265\tgone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n   266\tthe channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n   267\tleaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n   268\tthe retry cases above, `blocked` for `next`.\n   269\t\n   270\t**Channel loss.** A channel ends when its reader answers `end`: the\n   271\tprojection closed it (3.1), the inner stream of a pair ended (3.3), or the\n   272\tmedium is gone. The link then abandons its outstanding ids, reports each\n   273\tabandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n   274\tanswers later `cursor` and `next` `transport-error` with\n   275\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`, not retryable;\n   276\t`append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n   277\tthe channel is down. Filed answers are still returned. Reattachment is the\n   278\tcaller's policy, by `attach!` on the same descriptor.\n   279\t\n   280\t**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n   281\toperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n   282\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n   283\t\n   284\t**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote\n   285\tdescriptor's source declares plus `:closable`, learned from the attach\n   286\tprobe's answer (2.1); before that answer it declares\n   287\t`#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source turns out to\n   288\tlack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n   289\thandle-relative, as the contract's Concurrency section already makes every\n   290\toutcome: one perspective at one moment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never\n   291\tanswers `cursor-mismatch` or `invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only\n   292\trelayed, and `append!` never answers the source's `full`, which arrives on\n   293\tthe event writer.\n   294\t\n   295\t### 2.5 Loss and resend\n   296\t\n   297\tThe reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n   298\tmeans: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n   299\twhen the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n   300\t`k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n   301\tthree operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n   302\tor a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n   303\tdeduplication and correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an\n   304\tappend whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n   305\tevent writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   306\t:dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends.\n   307\t\n   308\tThere are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no\n   309\tresult retention at the answering peer. The cursor is the sequence number\n   310\tfor reads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel\n   311\tartifact.\n   312\t\n   313\t## 3. Channels\n   314\t\n   315\tA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n   316\tis ordered and reliable; the link reads the last as composition data for\n   317\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n   318\tper channel.\n   319\t\n   320\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   321\t\n   322\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` is the socket layer, and\n   323\ta channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n   324\tattachment model and identity; the adapter that deposits every event as\n   325\t`{:ws/attachment id :ws/event e :ws/value v}` on the medium the composition\n   326\twired; the writer handle, whose `append!` frames each value as one\n   327\t`{:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value v}` message in the negotiated codec profile\n   328\t(Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile); the bounded acceptance\n   329\thandoff with slot and acknowledgement; the resolution and lifecycle\n   330\tvocabulary. Ordered and reliable; frame budget unbounded.\n   331\t\n   332\t**New, the projection.** The mirror and the link speak raw request and\n   333\tanswer maps, so each channel end composes one step, `ws-project`, between\n   334\tthe ws deposit medium and the channel. The channel reader is a ring buffer\n   335\tthe composition wires; the channel writer is the ws handle itself.\n   336\t`ws-project` holds the reading cursor on the attachment's traffic medium,\n   337\tkeeps the events whose `:ws/attachment` names this channel, appends the\n   338\t`:ws/value` of each `:ws/payload` onto the ring buffer, and drops\n   339\t`:ws/error` diagnostics. A terminal lifecycle event for the attachment\n   340\t(`:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`) or a failure resolution (`:ws/not-found`,\n   341\t`:ws/transport-error`) makes `ws-project` close the ring buffer: channel\n   342\tloss is then the link's `end` observation (2.4). `ws-project` is a step the\n   343\tcomposition drives at its own cadence, as it drives `endpoint-step`.\n   344\t\n   345\tDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n   346\tacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n   347\tnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n   348\tcljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n   349\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n   350\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   351\t\n   352\t### 3.2 UDP\n   353\t\n   354\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   355\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   356\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   357\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired.\n   358\t\n   359\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   360\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   361\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   362\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   363\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n   364\t  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n   365\t  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n   366\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   367\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   368\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   369\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   370\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n   371\t  source-address, direction, id]`, because ids are unique only per channel\n   372\t  reader and one socket may serve several attachments, and delivers only a\n   373\t  complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole message,\n   374\t  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n   375\t  composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB)\n   376\t  and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n   377\t  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n   378\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   379\t  in a payload.\n   380\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   381\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   382\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   383\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   384\t  republishes.\n   385\t\n   386\tHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n   387\t(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n   388\t\n   389\t### 3.3 Pair\n   390\t\n   391\tA pair descriptor names two remote descriptors:\n   392\t\n   393\t```clojure\n   394\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/pair\n   395\t :dao.stream/identity  <channel identity>\n   396\t :dao.stream.remote/in  <remote descriptor>    ; this peer reads here\n   397\t :dao.stream.remote/out <remote descriptor>}   ; this peer writes here\n   398\t```\n   399\t\n   400\t`attach!` on it returns a channel whose reader is a reflection of `in` and\n   401\twhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\n   402\tstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\n   403\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   404\tbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\n   405\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   406\tunder the inboxes' retention. A `gap` observed on `in` is channel loss,\n   407\tabsorbed by the resend rule, and is never reported to an inner reader as\n   408\tthe source's `gap`; `end` on `in` is the channel's end (2.4). Double\n   409\tframing consumes budget: an inner value inside an outer `append!` must fit\n   410\tthe outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   410\tthe outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   411\t\n   412\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   413\t\n   414\tA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   415\tcoordinated simultaneous send. The protocol cannot change that; it makes\n   416\tthe workarounds conventions. One peer, M below, is reachable and serves two\n   417\tordinary streams from its table; which peer that is carries no protocol\n   418\tmeaning, and any reachable peer can take its place.\n   419\t\n   420\t- One side receives unsolicited (public address, forwarded port, or\n   421\t  endpoint-independent mapping and filtering): direct; the other side dials,\n   422\t  WebSocket or UDP.\n   423\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping or filtering on one or both sides, punching\n   424\t  still possible: hole punch through M (below), UDP allowed.\n   425\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a symmetric NAT;\n   426\t  the strict carrier-grade paths): punching is unreliable; relay through a\n   427\t  pair served by M.\n   428\t- UDP blocked: WebSocket only, to a reachable peer, and the browser rule\n   429\t  applies; without one, impossible (section 1).\n   430\t- Browser at one end: it dials, and two browsers relay through a pair. A\n   431\t  WebRTC data channel is a future channel type whose candidates ride the\n   432\t  same meeting stream.\n   433\t\n   434\t**Hole punching, as a convention.** M serves a `meet-requests` stream with\n   435\tsurface `#{:writer}` and a `meet-board` stream with surface `#{:reader}`,\n   436\tboth ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel a peer appends\n   437\t`{:meet/here <name>}`; M's interpreter reads the request and the channel\n   438\tattachment identity it arrived on, which for UDP is the reflexive address,\n   439\tand appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h :port p}\n   440\t:dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the board,\n   441\tthen each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\n   442\treflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\n   443\tanswer, even a `not-found` error, carrying one of this peer's outstanding\n   444\tids arrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone\n   445\tconfirms nothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe\n   446\tframe exists.\n   447\t\n   448\t**Bounded meeting work.** M's interpreter bounds its own work: the board's\n   449\tretention (composition data, evict-oldest by default), the number of active\n   450\tpairs in its table, and the fanout of one driver step. A meeting request M\n   451\tcannot honor within its bounds is refused by a gate on its `meet-requests`\n   452\tentry and observed by the asker as `:dao.stream/refused` through its\n   453\treflection: exhaustion is a present answer, not silence. A peer reachable\n   454\tthrough M is reachable only while its channel to M lives, so its keep-alive\n   455\tcadence is bounded by the NAT binding's lifetime, tens of seconds for UDP,\n   456\tlonger for TCP.\n   457\t\n   458\t**Relay, as a convention.** A peer asks M, through `meet-requests`, for a\n   459\tpair; M creates two ring buffers, enters them in its table under a lease,\n   460\tand posts their remote descriptors on the board; the asking peers attach a\n   461\tpair channel over them (3.3). M interprets nothing it relays, and that two\n   462\tunreachable peers need M at all is a fact about the network, not a role in\n   463\tthis protocol. Reflexive addresses on a board are readable by every reader\n   464\tof the board; this convention claims no private delivery.\n   465\t\n   466\t## 5. Conventions that are not protocol\n   467\t\n   468\tNone of these has a frame. Each is peers using sections 2 and 3.\n   469\t\n   470\t**The toy.** Peer B holds a handle `s` over the single value `\"hello\"`,\n   471\tidentity `\"str-1\"`, complete history, surface `#{:reader}`. B's table is\n   472\t`{\"str-1\" {:handle s :surface #{:reader}}}`. B accepts WebSocket connections\n   473\tas `dao.stream.ws.md` specifies and drives, per accepted connection,\n   474\t`ws-project` and then `mirror-step` over the projected reader and the\n   475\tsocket handle. B publishes `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote\n   476\t:dao.stream/identity \"str-1\" :dao.stream/channel <B's ws descriptor>}`.\n   477\tPeer A calls `attach!`; the link dials and sends the `descriptor` probe;\n   478\tthe answer carries `:dao.stream.remote/surface #{:reader}`. A calls\n   479\t`cursor` with `:oldest`: no answer filed, the link sends the request and\n   480\tanswers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`; on the next call\n   481\tthe drain files the source's `ok` with `c0`. `next c0` proceeds the same\n   482\tway: `blocked`, then `ok \"hello\" c1`, and `next c1` is `end`. Nothing knew\n   483\tit was a string.\n   484\t\n   485\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   486\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   487\tan interpreter over the local ends; a peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   488\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   489\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`. Request\n   490\tids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers` stream\n   491\tdo not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a pair. S is\n   492\ta \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its interpreter has\n   493\tthat policy, and either peer may serve such a pair to the other over the\n   494\tsame channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape `yin.repl` and\n   495\tcontent lookup take (section 8).\n   496\t\n   497\t**Meeting board and relay pair.** Section 4.\n   498\t\n   499\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-certifying hash of a public key, the\n   500\tkickoff-hash form of `dao.stream.discovery.md`. It appears only inside\n   501\tconvention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until authentication exists.\n   502\t\n   503\t## 6. Lease integration\n   504\t\n   505\tA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\n   506\tgoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\n   507\tpossesses the resource, as that contract requires. What a lease governs:\n   508\t\n   509\t- A table entry a peer serves by its own policy: no lease; the peer retires\n   510\t  it when it likes.\n   511\t- A table entry served for a remote party (a stream a migrated continuation\n   512\t  still reads): yes; the remote party holds it, the holding peer grants and\n   513\t  judges, the identity is the subject.\n   514\t- A relay pair on a meeting peer: yes; the requesting peer holds it, the\n   515\t  meeting peer grants and judges, the two identities are the subject.\n   516\t- Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets: no; governed by close\n   517\t  or by a bounded buffer.\n   518\t- NAT mappings and punch state: no; possessed by the NAT box, so no judge\n   519\t  can exist, and keep-alive cadence is the peer's own (section 4).\n   520\t- Board postings: no; records under the board's retention, each carrying the\n   521\t  lease id of the registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a\n   522\t  posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.\n   523\t\n   524\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   525\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   526\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   527\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   528\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   529\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   530\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   531\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   532\tholder by the medium, the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` names as\n   533\tper-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The renewal\n   534\tmedium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes the\n   535\tsubject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and reports\n   536\tsuccess. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n   537\t\n   538\tUntil ShiBi exists, a renewal medium is reachable by anyone who learns its\n   539\tdescriptor and accepts a third party's renewal, extending a lease it does\n   540\tnot hold: attribution needs no credential, extension may want one, and a\n   541\tcomposition that must not accept it composes a gate on the renewal entry.\n   542\t\n   543\t**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n   544\t:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\n   545\treflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\n   546\trenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\n   547\twriter. A holder with no event writer stops at its bound early, never late.\n   548\t\n   549\t**Reclaim observed.** The mirror answers the `not-found` protocol error for\n   550\ta reclaimed identity and the reflection marks itself gone (2.4). The holder\n   551\tmay also read the grantor's own `:lapsed` record through its reflection on\n   552\t`lease-grants`: that is observing the grantor's stream, not carriage. And\n   553\tthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n   554\t\n   555\t**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers or\n   556\tthe channel's `end` (2.4); the table entry persists, so a holder that\n   557\treconnects within duration plus tolerance finds its entry, its cursors and\n   558\tits lease intact. Expiry is a present answer, the `not-found` error, on a\n   559\thealthy channel. Only expiry frees anything. A meeting peer that restarts\n   560\tloses its table and ledger, so every pair is gone, holders observe\n   561\t`not-found` and re-propose; this is `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant\n   562\twith an empty inventory, correct because the table is process-scoped.\n   563\t\n   564\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`.\n   565\t\n   565\t\n   566\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   567\t\n   568\tThe mechanism is `dao.stream.middleware.md`: a middleware is a map of two\n   569\tpure transforms over the operation map and the outcome map; `wrap` turns a\n   570\thandle and a chain into a handle on the same logical stream; a middleware\n   571\tmaps position `p` of the inner to position `p` of the outer and never\n   572\ttouches a cursor, an anchor, an identity, an id or an outcome kind. This\n   573\tdocument adds the network attachment points: the **table entry** (the\n   574\tserved handle, before it enters the table; the mirror sees only the wrapped\n   575\thandle; gating, value encryption for serving, redaction, metering), the\n   576\t**reflection** (inside the host-composed attach closure; presenting a\n   577\tcredential, value decryption, metering), and the **channel** (reserved for\n   578\ta channel-level wrapper, deferred below).\n   579\t\n   580\tKeys and chains are composition data handed to `wrap`, never descriptor\n   581\tfields. Value-level encryption hides values and leaves identity, ops,\n   582\tcursors, sizes and timing visible. **Channel-level encryption is deferred:\n   583\tthis version specifies no wrapper at the channel attachment point**, its\n   584\tinterface and its ordering against codec and fragmentation (encode, then\n   585\tencrypt, then fragment) being requirements on that future design, not a\n   586\tdesign here. The exact consequence: a relay peer that carries requests and\n   587\tanswers sees every envelope in the clear, identities, ops, cursors, ids,\n   588\tsizes and timing included, and any part of a value not hidden by composed\n   589\tvalue-level middleware. Key distribution is a convention and is not\n   590\tspecified here. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   591\t\n   592\t**The seam.** Authentication and authorization belong to ShiBi\n   593\t(`dao.shibi.md`), unspecified. This document offers only the slot, a\n   594\treserved open key on the request map `:dao.stream.remote/credential` whose\n   595\tvalue is opaque here and travels per request (a per-channel credential is a\n   596\tconvention: the reflection's `present` middleware attaches the same value\n   597\tto every request); the context, `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <attachment\n   598\tidentity>}` handed by the mirror to `apply-request` while local callers\n   599\thand `{}` and no other context key exists in this version; the gate,\n   600\tmirror-side `dao.stream.middleware/gate`, whose `verify` is a pure function\n   601\tof the latest decision, context and request (the decisions are published to\n   602\ta capacity-1 medium by an index interpreter the composition runs over its\n   603\tfact streams) and whose refusal is the contract's `:dao.stream/refused`\n   604\twith an opaque `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`; and the presenter,\n   605\treflection-side `dao.stream.middleware/present`, a pure function of context\n   606\tand request that fills the slot.\n   607\t\n   608\tA ShiBi built as index and query interpreters over a tuple space plugs in\n   609\twithout changing this document: its index interpreter is the interpreter\n   610\tthat folds the streams it observes and publishes decisions onto the\n   611\tdecision medium, its query interpreter is the gate's `verify`, its tokens\n   612\tare the slot's values, and its decisions, where it meters, are the gate's\n   613\tdecision emissions onto a stream the index also observes. Limitation,\n   614\tstated plainly: the seam carries the index's latest decision per medium,\n   615\tnot the history it folded; a query that must reason over history folds it\n   616\tin the index and publishes a derived decision. Until ShiBi exists, gating\n   617\tis an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel` or a pre-shared\n   618\tvalue in the slot.\n   619\t\n   620\tLease attribution (section 6) rests on per-author media alone and needs no\n   621\tcredential; a gate on a renewal medium is an addition.\n   622\t\n   623\t## 8. Unification\n   624\t\n   625\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree, and its fate:\n   626\t\n   627\t- `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart adapters: **subsumed**.\n   628\t  Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium plus the 3.1 projection. Its\n   629\t  accept and disclaim frames and served-path table become unnecessary once\n   630\t  `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is a slice.\n   631\t- `dao.stream.serving`: **retired**. Forwards a source into every accepted\n   632\t  socket and flattens a source `gap` into a detach: the copy model. Its\n   633\t  consumers are `yin.repl.serve` and `dao.jing.remote` (which requires and\n   634\t  drives it); both are retired by the slices that follow.\n   635\t- `dao.stream.forward`: **unrelated**. A local copy step; replication by\n   636\t  convention, not exposure.\n   637\t- `dao.stream.rpc`: **convention-over**. Correlation by id over two streams\n   638\t  is section 5's service; its state machine may be kept as one such\n   639\t  vocabulary over reflections.\n   640\t- `dao.stream.rpc.ws`: **retired**. Its only job is translating `:ws/`\n   641\t  envelopes for rpc; the 3.1 projection is the channel binding now.\n   642\t- `dao.stream.apply` as a wire envelope: **retired**. Its ok-or-error\n   643\t  wrapper duplicates the outcome map. The `:dao.stream.apply/call` AST node\n   644\t  and the VM's FFI bridge keep the name and are unrelated to the network.\n   645\t- `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter`, the `daostream:ws://`\n   646\t  URL: **convention-over**. An eval service: `requests` and `answers`\n   647\t  streams, `:op/eval` as payload; the URL becomes a remote descriptor for\n   648\t  that pair. The shared-shell privilege is its interpreter's policy.\n   649\t- `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async`, the `:dao.jing/remote`\n   650\t  coordinate in `dao.jing.coordinate`: **retired**. Deprecated whole;\n   651\t  replaced by `dao.jing.content`, a request and response service (section\n   652\t  5) whose vocabulary, stepped client, driver, async facade and\n   653\t  two-descriptor coordinate are specified in the implementation plan.\n   654\t- `yin.repl.link`, `yin.vm.linker` M3 and M4: **convention-over**. The\n   655\t  content pair is a request and response service over reflections or local\n   656\t  ring buffers; the linker's stepped core is unchanged.\n   657\t- `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`:\n   658\t  **convention-over**. Consume the content client of the implementation\n   659\t  plan; the callback facade is host policy, per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n   660\t- `dao.jing.dht.node` UDP DHT: **unrelated**. Raw JVM datagram RPC with its\n   661\t  own Kademlia envelope; its socket rules and 1200-byte budget are the UDP\n   662\t  channel's template.\n   663\t- `dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`: **subsumed**. The channel\n   664\t  codecs, unchanged.\n   665\t\n   666\t## 9. Contract amendments\n   667\t\n   668\tIn `dao.stream.md`, five amendments and nothing network: OD-1 accepted with\n   669\tthe write fallback \"effect unknown; no automatic retry\" and the\n   670\t`:dao.stream/retry?` key; OD-2 accepted; OD-3 decision 2 accepted and (a)'s\n   671\tendpoint sentence in Envelopes; one `:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`,\n   672\t`next` and `append!`; one sentence in Surfaces on a handle composed over\n   673\tanother handle. These five are the whole of section 1's claim on the\n   674\tcontract. The companion edits the unification requires are inventoried in\n   675\tthe implementation plan.\n     1\t# DaoStream Middleware\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which wins on any disagreement. Every\n     5\tsentence below is a rule. This document names no network; the network\n     6\tattachment points are in [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md),\n     7\tsection 7.\n     8\t\n     9\t## What a middleware is\n    10\t\n    11\tA middleware is a value: a map of two pure transforms, one over the\n    12\toperation map on the way in and one over the outcome map on the way out. It\n    13\tis the adapter shape of `datom.world.md` (a map of transform functions)\n    14\tapplied to a handle rather than to a host boundary. Ring's request and\n    15\tresponse pair maps onto the operation map and the contract's outcome map.\n    16\t\n    17\t```clojure\n    18\t{:dao.stream.middleware/in  (fn [ctx req] -> req | outcome-map)\n    19\t :dao.stream.middleware/out (fn [ctx req outcome] -> outcome)}\n    20\t```\n    21\t\n    22\t`in` returning an outcome map short-circuits: the inner handle is not\n    23\tconsulted and that map is the operation's outcome, after the `out`\n    24\ttransforms of the middlewares outside it. `ctx` is a plain map the caller\n    25\tsupplies and no middleware may fabricate; a local caller supplies `{}`.\n    26\t\n    27\t## The operation map\n    28\t\n    29\tAn operation as data. Its vocabulary is `dao.stream.remote.md`'s so that the\n    30\tsame chain runs on either side of a channel without translation:\n    31\t\n    32\t```clojure\n    33\t{:dao.stream.remote/op    :dao.stream/cursor | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    34\t :dao.stream.remote/args  [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    35\t ...}                     ; open: further qualified keys pass through untouched\n    36\t```\n    37\t\n    38\t`descriptor` and `close!` are not operations a middleware sees: `descriptor`\n    39\tis a name and `close!` is a lifecycle transition, and neither has an outcome\n    40\ta policy may decline (`dao.stream.md`, Close; Envelopes).\n    41\t\n    42\t`(apply-request h ctx req) -> outcome` applies one operation map to a\n    43\thandle. It is the one place an operation map becomes a protocol call.\n    44\t\n    45\t## wrap\n    46\t\n    47\t`(wrap h chain) -> handle`, where `chain` is a vector of middlewares\n    48\tapplied outermost first. The returned handle implements the protocols the\n    49\tinner handle implements and no others. On each protocol call it builds the\n    50\toperation map, runs every `in` from the outermost inward, applies the result\n    51\tto `h` with `apply-request`, and runs every `out` from the innermost\n    52\toutward. Its `descriptor` and `close!` delegate to `h` unchanged.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe wrapped handle is a handle on the same logical stream as `h`: it\n    55\tdeclares `h`'s identity, passes cursors through, and may present values\n    56\tunder its own interpretation (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n    57\t\n    58\t## The position rule\n    59\t\n    60\tA middleware maps position `p` of the inner handle to position `p` of the\n    61\touter handle, for every `p`. It therefore never:\n    62\t\n    63\t- constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or a `gap` recovery\n    64\t  cursor;\n    65\t- changes `:dao.stream/outcome`, `:dao.stream/identity`,\n    66\t  `:dao.stream.remote/op` or `:dao.stream.remote/id`;\n    67\t- skips, merges or reorders elements.\n    68\t\n    69\tIt may replace `(first args)` on `append!` and `:dao.stream/value` on an\n    70\t`ok` from `next` with any value, of any size; add or read open keys; and\n    71\tshort-circuit with an outcome from the operation's exhaustive set. A cipher,\n    72\ta compressor, a redactor and an annotator preserve the rule. A filter that\n    73\tdrops elements does not: it is an interpreter that forwards into a new\n    74\tstream with its own identity (`dao.stream.md`, Composition), never a\n    75\tmiddleware.\n    76\t\n    77\t## The four prohibitions\n    78\t\n    79\tA transform is total and returns at once. It never loops over any handle,\n    80\tnever retries, never waits, and never touches the inner handle: the only\n    81\thandle operations it may perform are the decision read `gate` defines below\n    82\t(at most two `next` on a composed decision medium) and one `append!` to a\n    83\tside stream it was composed with. It reads no host clock. A transform that\n    84\tneeds facts from a stream is not a middleware: folding a stream is an\n    85\tinterpreter's work, and what a middleware reads is a decision an\n    86\tinterpreter already published onto a medium.\n    87\t\n    88\t## Failure inside a transform\n    89\t\n    90\tA value that cannot be transformed on the way out (undecodable ciphertext,\n    91\ta corrupt compressed value) is presented as\n    92\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/undecodable true :dao.stream.middleware/raw v}`\n    93\tunder `:dao.stream/value`, with the outcome and cursor untouched: the\n    94\tposition is real and the reader decides. A value that cannot be transformed\n    95\ton the way in answers `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.\n    96\t\n    97\t## Two generic middlewares\n    98\t\n    99\t**gate**, the authorize step. Composition data only; the policy is not\n   100\tspecified here.\n   101\t\n   102\t```clojure\n   103\t(gate {:dao.stream.middleware/verify   (fn [decision ctx req] -> nil | reason) ; pure; nil allows\n   104\t       :dao.stream.middleware/decision h}) ; a capacity-1 evict-oldest medium of decisions\n   105\t```\n   106\t\n   107\tThe decision medium is written by an interpreter the composition runs over\n   108\tits fact streams (the index): it folds facts with its own cursors, at its\n   109\town cadence, and appends each derived decision. Neither the gate nor the\n   110\tindex knows of the other; the medium is their only coupling.\n   111\t\n   112\tThe gate's `in` performs the decision read: one `next` at the reading\n   113\tcursor `wrap` keeps on the medium; a `gap` adopts its recovery cursor and\n   114\treads once more, which on a capacity-1 medium is the latest decision;\n   115\t`blocked` and `end` read nothing. `wrap` also keeps the last decision\n   116\tread, so `verify`'s first argument is the latest decision observed at or\n   117\tbefore this call, or the marker `{:dao.stream.middleware/none true}`\n   118\tbefore any. `nil` passes the request through; a reason short-circuits\n   119\twith `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused\n   120\t:dao.stream.middleware/reason reason}`. A policy that must fail closed\n   121\ttreats the marker as its reason. The reading cursor and the last decision\n   122\tadvance only inside a call, in the caller's control flow; they and the\n   123\tmedium are arguments to `wrap`, so this is handle-held state like a\n   124\ttransport constructor's closure, not hidden or shared state. The medium\n   125\tis not the wrapped stream: the position rule governs the inner handle's\n   126\telements and is untouched.\n   127\t\n   128\tA gate that must account for its own decisions appends each as\n   129\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\n   130\tit was composed with: this is **decision emission**, and an index that\n   131\tobserves that stream is how metered use keeps its state on a stream\n   132\trather than in the gate.\n   133\t\n   134\t**present**, the credential step.\n   135\t\n   136\t```clojure\n   137\t(present {:dao.stream.middleware/present (fn [ctx req] -> credential)})\n   138\t```\n   139\t\n   140\tIts `in` associates the result under `:dao.stream.remote/credential`. Its\n   141\t`out` is identity.\n   142\t\n   143\tNeither middleware knows what a credential is. The trivial policy this\n   144\trepository ships with is an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel`\n   145\tin `ctx`, ignoring the credential. Everything else belongs to\n   146\t[`dao.shibi.md`](./dao.shibi.md).\n   147\t\n   148\t## Exemplars\n   149\t\n   150\tValue encryption: `in` on `:dao.stream/append!` replaces the value with its\n   151\tauthenticated ciphertext; `out` on an `ok` from `:dao.stream/next` replaces\n   152\t`:dao.stream/value` with the plaintext or the undecodable marker. Cursors\n   153\tuntouched. The key is an argument to the constructor that built the\n   154\tmiddleware, exactly as a deposit medium is an argument to a transport\n   155\tconstructor. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   156\t\n   157\tMetering: `out` appends `{:meter/identity id :meter/op op :meter/outcome\n   158\tkind}` to a composed metering stream and returns the outcome unchanged. The\n   159\tside effect is a stream emission (`datom.world.md`, Streams).\n   160\t\n   161\t## Invariant compliance\n   162\t\n   163\tNo callback: a transform is called downward by the caller in the caller's\n   164\tcontrol flow and returns a value; nothing is registered to be invoked\n   165\tlater. No implicit control flow: the four prohibitions. No hidden global\n   166\tstate: keys, policies and decision media are arguments to `wrap`. No shared\n   167\tmutable state beyond what any handle holds. Interpretation and execution\n   168\tstay separate: `apply-request` judges no value. No assumed graph: a chain is\n   169\tan explicit vector.\n     1\t# ShiBi\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: stub. ShiBi is not specified and not implemented\n     4\t(`docs/bootstrap.md`, Capability Engine). This page records only what other\n     5\tdocuments may rely on and what ShiBi must supply. It designs nothing.\n     6\t\n     7\t## What it is\n     8\t\n     9\tShiBi is the capability system of datom.world: authentication and\n    10\tauthorization are its job and no other document's. It is to be built the\n    11\tway `dao.space` is built, as a tuple space that emerges from two\n    12\tinterpreters over ordinary streams, one that indexes and one that queries,\n    13\tneither knowing the other exists (`datom.world.md`, Streams). Its tokens are\n    14\tdescribed elsewhere as Macaroon-style: attenuatable, offline-verifiable,\n    15\trevocable caveats (`dao.space.security.md`, Capabilities Govern\n    16\tInterpreters).\n    17\t\n    18\t## How it plugs in\n    19\t\n    20\tShiBi integrates through the middleware seam of\n    21\t[`dao.stream.middleware.md`](./dao.stream.middleware.md) and the attachment\n    22\tpoints of [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md) section 7, which\n    23\tare fixed and which ShiBi must not change. To plug in it supplies:\n    24\t\n    25\t- a `verify`, a pure function of the latest decision, context and request\n    26\t  that answers nil or a reason: its query interpreter;\n    27\t- an index interpreter over its fact streams, folding with its own cursors\n    28\t  and publishing decisions onto a decision medium a gate reads\n    29\t  (`dao.stream.middleware.md`, gate);\n    30\t- a `present`, a pure function of context and request that produces the\n    31\t  value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`;\n    32\t- a reason vocabulary under its own namespace for\n    33\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`;\n    34\t- where use is metered, the decision facts it emits and indexes.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe contract's refusal outcome is `:dao.stream/refused` and is\n    37\tcapability-free (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention). Lease attribution\n    38\trests on per-author media and does not depend on ShiBi\n    39\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6; `yin.repl.link-policy.md`).\n    40\t\n    41\t## What blocks it being load-bearing\n    42\t\n    43\t`dao.stream.discovery.md`, The ShiBi precondition: ShiBi is a capability\n    44\ttoken in one place and a fungible currency in another, and a currency needs\n    45\tdouble-spend resolution, which is global consensus. Until that decision is\n    46\tmade, no design may depend on ShiBi for anything beyond the seam above, and\n    47\tgating in this repository is an allow-list or a pre-shared value.\n     1\t# DaoStream Remote Implementation Plan\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: plan, not rules. The rules are\n     4\t[`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md); this file carries the\n     5\tmigration design, the completion criteria and slices, and the companion-edit\n     6\tinventory moved out of that spec to keep it short. Subordinate to the spec\n     7\ton any disagreement.\n     8\t\n     9\t## 1. What replaces `dao.jing.remote`: `dao.jing.content`\n    10\t\n    11\tContent lookup is a request and response service over remote streams\n    12\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 5), in one module family, `dao.jing.content`:\n    13\tthe namespaces `dao.jing.content` (`serve-step`), `dao.jing.content.step`\n    14\t(`step`), `dao.jing.content.driver` (`driver`, clj only) and\n    15\t`dao.jing.content.async` (`async`).\n    16\t\n    17\t- **Vocabulary**, a payload convention: `{:jing/request r :jing/get\n    18\t  address}` answered by `{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64}`;\n    19\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}` answered by\n    20\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}`. `r` is a\n    21\t  self-minted random value. Bytes travel as padded standard Base64 text in\n    22\t  the application value; message boundaries are the channel codec's, one\n    23\t  application value per message, so the vocabulary rides Transit-JSON text\n    24\t  or canonical CBOR alike (`dao.jing.cbor.md`, Remote and DHT).\n    25\t- **The interpreter** (`dao.jing.content/serve-step`): a pure step over a\n    26\t  local `requests` reader and `answers` writer against a `dao.jing` handle.\n    27\t  It runs the one ingress canonicality check on every put.\n    28\t- **The ingress check**, one shared function `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`,\n    29\t  moved from today's private `dao.jing.remote/accept-bytes!` and used by\n    30\t  the interpreter, by the stepped client on every `get` answer and\n    31\t  `:present` verify, and by `yin.vm.linker`. The three copies become one\n    32\t  (today: `dao/jing/remote.cljc`, `dao/jing/remote/step.cljc`, and the\n    33\t  linker's own; only the first includes the canonical CBOR decode).\n    34\t- **The stepped client** (`dao.jing.content.step`): the `request-put`,\n    35\t  `request-get`, `request-materialize`, `step`, `abandon` shape of\n    36\t  `dao.jing.remote.step`, over a `requests` writer and an `answers` reader\n    37\t  that are reflections or local handles. This is the portable interface,\n    38\t  per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n    39\t- **The blocking driver** (`dao.jing.content.driver`, clj only): host\n    40\t  policy that steps the client and sleeps, returning a synchronous\n    41\t  `dao.jing` handle. It replaces `connect-content!`; its consumers are the\n    42\t  coordinate opener and, through it, `dao.space` (the published-index\n    43\t  coordinate opens it at `src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc`) and `yin.repl.link`\n    44\t  on the JVM. `yin.vm.content` has no remote path today (its handle is an\n    45\t  argument), so it gains one only by being handed a coordinate-built handle.\n    46\t- **The async facade** (`dao.jing.content.async`): replaces\n    47\t  `dao.jing.remote.async` over the new stepped client, same consumer API;\n    48\t  `dao.data.btree.storage` is the consumer (`hydrate-async`,\n    49\t  `store-tree-async`).\n    50\t- **The coordinate**: `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests\n    51\t  <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` on every\n    52\t  host, resolved by `dao.jing.coordinate/open!` to the driver on clj and to\n    53\t  the stepped client elsewhere. The `:url` form is gone; its consumers are\n    54\t  the tests and the coordinate itself.\n    55\t\n    56\t## 2. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n    57\t\n    58\tOrdered. Each names files, host lanes and its proof. Verification commands\n    59\tare in `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`.\n    60\t\n    61\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    62\t| #  | Slice and files                                            | Proves                                                       | Lanes         |\n    63\t+====+============================================================+==============================================================+===============+\n    64\t| 0  | Contract and source alignment: the `dao.stream.md`         | Existing consumers pass; `dao.stream.observe/step` and the   | clj cljs cljd |\n    65\t|    | amendments of the spec's section 9 are accepted;           | engine treat an unrecognized outcome as refused, not a       |               |\n    66\t|    | `:dao.stream/refused` added to the closed outcome sets     | throw; the outcome sets admit the refusal.                   |               |\n    67\t|    | for `cursor`, `next` and `append!` in                      |                                                              |               |\n    68\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`; `dao.stream.observe`,          |                                                              |               |\n    69\t|    | `yin.vm.engine` follow the unrecognized-outcome rule.      |                                                              |               |\n    70\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    71\t| 1  | `dao.stream.middleware`: `apply-request`, `wrap`, `gate`,  | Position rule under a value cipher over a ring buffer; a     | clj cljs cljd |\n    72\t|    | `present`, a metering exemplar, an index-interpreter        | gate with the channel allow-list refuses with                |               |\n    73\t|    | exemplar publishing decisions. `src/cljc/dao/stream/        | `:dao.stream/refused`; a gate reads the latest decision      |               |\n    74\t|    | middleware.cljc`, tests.                                   | through a capacity-1 medium after eviction; a filter cannot  |               |\n    75\t|    |                                                            | be expressed.                                                |               |\n    76\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    77\t| 2  | `dao.stream.remote` core: `mirror-step`, link, reflection, | The toy over two in-process ring buffers as the channel;     | clj cljs cljd |\n    78\t|    | remote descriptor dispatch, the protocol errors.           | `gap` from an evicting source crosses verbatim with the      |               |\n    79\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`, tests.                  | source's cursor; `blocked` then `ok` on re-ask; `not-found`  |               |\n    80\t|    |                                                            | marks gone; `no-surface` does not; the descriptor answer     |               |\n    81\t|    |                                                            | carries the surface; a kept cursor from a reflection is      |               |\n    82\t|    |                                                            | accepted by the source handle.                               |               |\n    83\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    84\t| 3  | WebSocket channel composition: `ws-project` at each end;   | The toy across a socket on clj to Node, Node to clj, cljd    | clj cljs cljd |\n    85\t|    | mirror steps over accepted and dialed attachments;         | to clj; both directions serve on one channel; browser        |               |\n    86\t|    | retire `:ws/accept`, disclaim and the served-path table    | dials and reads; a closed connection is observed as the      |               |\n    87\t|    | in `dao.stream.ws`.                                        | link's `end`.                                                |               |\n    88\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    89\t| 4  | Content service: the `dao.jing.content` family (section 1) | `dao.jing.remote` tests ported and passing over the new      | clj cljs cljd |\n    90\t|    | with `serve-step`, `step`, `driver` (clj), `async`;         | module; `dao.space.index`, btree hydration, the linker M3    |               |\n    91\t|    | `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`; `dao.jing.coordinate` new         | and M4 tests unchanged in outcome; no require of             |               |\n    92\t|    | coordinate; delete `src/cljc/dao/jing/remote.cljc`,        | `dao.jing.remote` remains.                                   |               |\n    93\t|    | `remote/step.cljc`, `remote/async.cljc` and their tests.   |                                                              |               |\n    94\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    95\t| 5  | REPL as a service: `yin.repl.serve` and `connect` over     | `(connect \"daostream:ws://...\")` resolves to a remote        | clj cljs cljd |\n    96\t|    | `requests` and `answers` reflections; delete               | descriptor pair; the demo REPLs work; `yin.repl.link` serves |               |\n    97\t|    | `dao.stream.serving`, `dao.stream.rpc.ws`, the apply wire  | content through slice 4; `dao.stream.rpc` is reworked over   |               |\n    98\t|    | envelope from `dao.stream.apply`; rework or retire         | reflections or retired in the same slice, because the apply  |               |\n    99\t|    | `dao.stream.rpc`.                                          | envelope it requires is deleted here.                        |               |\n   100\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   101\t| 6  | UDP channel: `dao.stream.udp` with fragmentation keyed by  | The toy over UDP with a 100 KiB value; loss injection       | clj cljs cljd |\n   102\t|    | attachment, address, direction and id; adapters            | recovers by resend; oversize beyond the maximum answers      |               |\n   103\t|    | `src/clj/dao/stream/udp/jvm.clj`, `src/cljs/dao/           | `transport-error` with reason `oversize`.                    |               |\n   104\t|    | stream/udp/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/udp/dart.cljd`.|                                                              |               |\n   105\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   106\t| 7  | Pair channel and the meeting and relay conventions with    | Two peers behind a simulated restricted NAT punch; behind    | clj           |\n   107\t|    | leases: `dao.stream.remote.pair`, `dao.stream.remote.meet`;| a simulated symmetric NAT they relay; a pair whose holder    |               |\n   108\t|    | a meeting gate refuses past the pair bound.                | stops renewing answers `not-found` after duration plus       |               |\n   109\t|    |                                                            | tolerance; a reconnect within it finds the pair intact; a    |               |\n   110\t|    |                                                            | meeting peer past its bound refuses, not silences.          |               |\n   111\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   112\t| 8  | UCF facade: `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`      | The acceptance row: a string-backed stream migrates with a   | clj cljs cljd |\n   113\t|    | 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 lift and lower through remote descriptors  | kept cursor and resumes; forced eviction yields `gap` with   |               |\n   114\t|    | with the `:dao.stream.remote/v1` cursor profile.           | the source's cursor.                                         |               |\n   115\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n   116\t\n   117\tDeferred, in order of expected arrival: authentication and authorization\n   118\t(ShiBi), key distribution, channel-level encryption, a WebRTC data channel,\n   119\tbrowser inbound. None changes a shape in the spec's section 2.\n   120\t\n   121\t## 3. Companion-edit inventory\n   122\t\n   123\tThe edits the unification requires in other design documents, beyond the\n   124\tspec's own sections. Those not yet made are slices; those made in the\n   125\tauthoring and fix rounds are listed as done.\n   126\t\n   127\t- `dao.stream.md` (done): OD-1, OD-2, OD-3 accepted and moved into the\n   128\t  contract body; the `:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`, `next` and\n   129\t  `append!`; the composed-handle sentence in Surfaces; the OD-3 wording\n   130\t  corrected so `attach!`'s `not-found` and a stale cursor are separate\n   131\t  cases.\n   132\t- `dao.lease.md` (done): served entries and relay pairs named as lease\n   133\t  subjects; the carriage note names the `not-found` protocol error.\n   134\t- `dao.stream.ws.md` (done): the stream belongs to the peer that holds it;\n   135\t  the copy path versus attachment to the original; dialer-side and\n   136\t  acceptor-side; deferred liveness, resumption and RPC items point to the\n   137\t  spec.\n   138\t- `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (done): 7.4.3 and 7.5.3 cite\n   139\t  the spec; the put resume rule waits on the event writer's source outcome;\n   140\t  `:yin.k/cursor-profiles` names `:dao.stream.remote/v1`; the acceptance\n   141\t  row cites slice 8 here.\n   142\t- `dao.jing.md` (done): the coordinate shape and its today/target split;\n   143\t  the deprecated backend bullet; `dao.jing/accept-bytes!` marked as the\n   144\t  target; the Base64 carriage named as channel-codec message boundaries.\n   145\t- `yin.vm.linker.md` (done): the content pair vocabulary cites section 1\n   146\t  here; the 6.1 table row, 6.3 shape, M3, deps and failure-policy timing.\n   147\t- `dao.data.btree.md` (done): the 5.4 backend row annotated as the target\n   148\t  name for `dao.jing.remote`; the Phase 4 landing note.\n   149\t- `dao.jing.dht.md` (done): the NAT limitation.\n   150\t- `daostream-udp-design.md` (done): superseded status line.\n   151\t- `dao.jing.cbor.md` (done): the framing claims corrected to channel-codec\n   152\t  message boundaries with Base64 in the application value.\n   153\t- `yin.vm.debruijn.linker.md`, `dao.space.query.md`,\n   154\t  `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `dao.jing.remote.implementation-plan.md`\n   155\t  (done): one status sentence each naming the successor.\n   156\t- `dao.jing.hash-registry.md`, `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md`\n   157\t  (done): one-line pointers that the `dao.jing.remote` sites describe\n   158\t  today's code and the successor keeps the checks and classifications.\n   159\t- Remaining, with the code: the `dao.stream.ws.md` residual dialer/acceptor\n   160\t  vocabulary beside its older deposit-model wording, and `dao.stream.md`'s\n   161\t  own Close-section \"server-side accepted-connection handle\", track the\n   162\t  contract's wording and are re-worded with the code slices, not before.\n   510\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   511\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   512\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   513\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   514\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   515\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   516\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   517\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   518\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   519\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, `:dao.stream/refused`, and\n   520\t  `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   521\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   522\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   523\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   524\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   525\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   526\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   527\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   528\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   529\t  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n   530\t  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n   531\t  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n   532\t  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n   533\t- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n   534\t  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n   535\t  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n   536\t  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n   537\t  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n   538\t  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n   539\t  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n   540\t  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n   541\t  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n   542\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   543\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   544\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   545\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   546\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   547\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   548\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. A cursor is plain data and\n   549\t  survives the host serialization codec structurally unchanged, under the\n   550\t  same rule as the envelopes. A cursor that outlives the stream it names is\n   551\t  a cursor like any other: `next` answers `cursor-mismatch` when the handle\n   552\t  it is given is on a different logical stream, never a defect. A descriptor\n   553\t  whose stream no longer exists is `attach!`'s own `not-found`, a separate\n   554\t  case that takes no cursor.\n   555\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   556\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   557\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   558\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   559\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   560\t  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n    25\tthan \"backend\" vs. \"not a backend\":** `dao.jing.file` is the only backend\n    26\tthat re-ingests its own output across process death — it must persist an\n    27\tappend-only log and later replay it with no external message boundary to\n    28\tmark records, so it alone must invent a self-describing frame, which is\n    29\twhy that frame is itself a CBOR structure (a two-element\n    30\t`[digest payload-bytes]` array; see *Memory and files*). `dao.jing.mem`\n    31\tnever persists, so it has no framing at all; `dao.jing.content` (formerly\n    32\t`dao.jing.remote`)/`dao.jing.dht`\n    33\tget record boundaries from the channel codec's own message framing, one\n    34\tapplication value per message: Transit-JSON text or canonical CBOR binary\n    35\ton a WebSocket channel, canonical CBOR on the UDP channel\n    36\t(`dao.stream.remote.md` 3.1, 3.2), one Transit-JSON value per DHT datagram.\n    37\tSo payload bytes travel as Base64 in the application value, never a raw\n    38\tCBOR array. This is not\n    39\tabout `dao.jing.file` being more or less \"Jing's own\" than any other\n    40\tbackend in this source tree — all of `memory`/`file`/`remote`/`dht` are\n    41\tequally in-repo; only PostgreSQL and S3 are genuinely third-party and not\n    42\tyet built. What is third-party-implementable is the byte-store *contract*\n   425\tselects a different value codec or re-hashes a host-specific representation\n   426\t— with one named exception, `dao.jing.file`'s own frame parsing (see the\n   427\tObjective's carve-out, and *Memory and files* below).\n   428\t\n   429\t### Memory and files\n   430\t\n   431\tThe memory backend stores byte snapshots under content addresses. The file\n   432\tbackend retains its append-only length framing, durability rules, duplicate\n   433\tvalidation, and torn-tail recovery. Each frame contains a CBOR\n   434\t`[digest payload-bytes]` record: digest is the raw digest byte string for\n   435\tthe address's carried algorithm (both initial registry algorithms are 32\n   436\tbytes) and payload-bytes contains the exact canonical payload.\n   437\t**`dao.jing.file` itself owns parsing and constructing this two-element\n   438\tframe**, with Jing's own shared codec — the exception the Objective names\n   439\tabove, needed because `dao.jing.file`'s frame shape is itself a CBOR\n   440\tstructure, unlike `dao.jing.mem`'s unframed map or the network backends'\n   441\tBase64-in-application-value carriage on channel-codec message boundaries\n   442\t(see the Objective). The\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '503,565p;565,630p;630,675p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '1,60p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '270,310p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '56,120p'; rg -n \\\"channel attachment identity|decision|capacity-1|one next|last-decision|fold|ShiBi|shibi\\\" docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/dao.shibi.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; rg -n \\\"not-found|no-surface|protocol error|append-unknown\\\" docs/design/dao.lease.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '1,180p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '503,565p;565,630p;630,675p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.shibi.md | sed -n '1,60p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.lease.md | sed -n '270,310p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '56,120p'; rg -n \\\"channel attachment identity|decision|capacity-1|one next|last-decision|fold|ShiBi|shibi\\\" docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md docs/design/dao.shibi.md docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; rg -n \\\"not-found|no-surface|protocol error|append-unknown\\\" docs/design/dao.lease.md\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Middleware\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which wins on any disagreement. Every\n     5\tsentence below is a rule. This document names no network; the network\n     6\tattachment points are in [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md),\n     7\tsection 7.\n     8\t\n     9\t## What a middleware is\n    10\t\n    11\tA middleware is a value: a map of two pure transforms, one over the\n    12\toperation map on the way in and one over the outcome map on the way out. It\n    13\tis the adapter shape of `datom.world.md` (a map of transform functions)\n    14\tapplied to a handle rather than to a host boundary. Ring's request and\n    15\tresponse pair maps onto the operation map and the contract's outcome map.\n    16\t\n    17\t```clojure\n    18\t{:dao.stream.middleware/in  (fn [ctx req] -> req | outcome-map)\n    19\t :dao.stream.middleware/out (fn [ctx req outcome] -> outcome)}\n    20\t```\n    21\t\n    22\t`in` returning an outcome map short-circuits: the inner handle is not\n    23\tconsulted and that map is the operation's outcome, after the `out`\n    24\ttransforms of the middlewares outside it. `ctx` is a plain map the caller\n    25\tsupplies and no middleware may fabricate; a local caller supplies `{}`.\n    26\t\n    27\t## The operation map\n    28\t\n    29\tAn operation as data. Its vocabulary is `dao.stream.remote.md`'s so that the\n    30\tsame chain runs on either side of a channel without translation:\n    31\t\n    32\t```clojure\n    33\t{:dao.stream.remote/op    :dao.stream/cursor | :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    34\t :dao.stream.remote/args  [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    35\t ...}                     ; open: further qualified keys pass through untouched\n    36\t```\n    37\t\n    38\t`descriptor` and `close!` are not operations a middleware sees: `descriptor`\n    39\tis a name and `close!` is a lifecycle transition, and neither has an outcome\n    40\ta policy may decline (`dao.stream.md`, Close; Envelopes).\n    41\t\n    42\t`(apply-request h ctx req) -> outcome` applies one operation map to a\n    43\thandle. It is the one place an operation map becomes a protocol call.\n    44\t\n    45\t## wrap\n    46\t\n    47\t`(wrap h chain) -> handle`, where `chain` is a vector of middlewares\n    48\tapplied outermost first. The returned handle implements the protocols the\n    49\tinner handle implements and no others. On each protocol call it builds the\n    50\toperation map, runs every `in` from the outermost inward, applies the result\n    51\tto `h` with `apply-request`, and runs every `out` from the innermost\n    52\toutward. Its `descriptor` and `close!` delegate to `h` unchanged.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe wrapped handle is a handle on the same logical stream as `h`: it\n    55\tdeclares `h`'s identity, passes cursors through, and may present values\n    56\tunder its own interpretation (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n    57\t\n    58\t## The position rule\n    59\t\n    60\tA middleware maps position `p` of the inner handle to position `p` of the\n    61\touter handle, for every `p`. It therefore never:\n    62\t\n    63\t- constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or a `gap` recovery\n    64\t  cursor;\n    65\t- changes `:dao.stream/outcome`, `:dao.stream/identity`,\n    66\t  `:dao.stream.remote/op` or `:dao.stream.remote/id`;\n    67\t- skips, merges or reorders elements.\n    68\t\n    69\tIt may replace `(first args)` on `append!` and `:dao.stream/value` on an\n    70\t`ok` from `next` with any value, of any size; add or read open keys; and\n    71\tshort-circuit with an outcome from the operation's exhaustive set. A cipher,\n    72\ta compressor, a redactor and an annotator preserve the rule. A filter that\n    73\tdrops elements does not: it is an interpreter that forwards into a new\n    74\tstream with its own identity (`dao.stream.md`, Composition), never a\n    75\tmiddleware.\n    76\t\n    77\t## The four prohibitions\n    78\t\n    79\tA transform is total and returns at once. It never loops over any handle,\n    80\tnever retries, never waits, and never touches the inner handle: the only\n    81\thandle operations it may perform are the decision read `gate` defines below\n    82\t(at most two `next` on a composed decision medium) and one `append!` to a\n    83\tside stream it was composed with. It reads no host clock. A transform that\n    84\tneeds facts from a stream is not a middleware: folding a stream is an\n    85\tinterpreter's work, and what a middleware reads is a decision an\n    86\tinterpreter already published onto a medium.\n    87\t\n    88\t## Failure inside a transform\n    89\t\n    90\tA value that cannot be transformed on the way out (undecodable ciphertext,\n    91\ta corrupt compressed value) is presented as\n    92\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/undecodable true :dao.stream.middleware/raw v}`\n    93\tunder `:dao.stream/value`, with the outcome and cursor untouched: the\n    94\tposition is real and the reader decides. A value that cannot be transformed\n    95\ton the way in answers `:dao.stream/invalid-value`.\n    96\t\n    97\t## Two generic middlewares\n    98\t\n    99\t**gate**, the authorize step. Composition data only; the policy is not\n   100\tspecified here.\n   101\t\n   102\t```clojure\n   103\t(gate {:dao.stream.middleware/verify   (fn [decision ctx req] -> nil | reason) ; pure; nil allows\n   104\t       :dao.stream.middleware/decision h}) ; a capacity-1 evict-oldest medium of decisions\n   105\t```\n   106\t\n   107\tThe decision medium is written by an interpreter the composition runs over\n   108\tits fact streams (the index): it folds facts with its own cursors, at its\n   109\town cadence, and appends each derived decision. Neither the gate nor the\n   110\tindex knows of the other; the medium is their only coupling.\n   111\t\n   112\tThe gate's `in` performs the decision read: one `next` at the reading\n   113\tcursor `wrap` keeps on the medium; a `gap` adopts its recovery cursor and\n   114\treads once more, which on a capacity-1 medium is the latest decision;\n   115\t`blocked` and `end` read nothing. `wrap` also keeps the last decision\n   116\tread, so `verify`'s first argument is the latest decision observed at or\n   117\tbefore this call, or the marker `{:dao.stream.middleware/none true}`\n   118\tbefore any. `nil` passes the request through; a reason short-circuits\n   119\twith `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused\n   120\t:dao.stream.middleware/reason reason}`. A policy that must fail closed\n   121\ttreats the marker as its reason. The reading cursor and the last decision\n   122\tadvance only inside a call, in the caller's control flow; they and the\n   123\tmedium are arguments to `wrap`, so this is handle-held state like a\n   124\ttransport constructor's closure, not hidden or shared state. The medium\n   125\tis not the wrapped stream: the position rule governs the inner handle's\n   126\telements and is untouched.\n   127\t\n   128\tA gate that must account for its own decisions appends each as\n   129\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/decision :allow | :refuse ...}` to a side stream\n   130\tit was composed with: this is **decision emission**, and an index that\n   131\tobserves that stream is how metered use keeps its state on a stream\n   132\trather than in the gate.\n   133\t\n   134\t**present**, the credential step.\n   135\t\n   136\t```clojure\n   137\t(present {:dao.stream.middleware/present (fn [ctx req] -> credential)})\n   138\t```\n   139\t\n   140\tIts `in` associates the result under `:dao.stream.remote/credential`. Its\n   141\t`out` is identity.\n   142\t\n   143\tNeither middleware knows what a credential is. The trivial policy this\n   144\trepository ships with is an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel`\n   145\tin `ctx`, ignoring the credential. Everything else belongs to\n   146\t[`dao.shibi.md`](./dao.shibi.md).\n   147\t\n   148\t## Exemplars\n   149\t\n   150\tValue encryption: `in` on `:dao.stream/append!` replaces the value with its\n   151\tauthenticated ciphertext; `out` on an `ok` from `:dao.stream/next` replaces\n   152\t`:dao.stream/value` with the plaintext or the undecodable marker. Cursors\n   153\tuntouched. The key is an argument to the constructor that built the\n   154\tmiddleware, exactly as a deposit medium is an argument to a transport\n   155\tconstructor. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   156\t\n   157\tMetering: `out` appends `{:meter/identity id :meter/op op :meter/outcome\n   158\tkind}` to a composed metering stream and returns the outcome unchanged. The\n   159\tside effect is a stream emission (`datom.world.md`, Streams).\n   160\t\n   161\t## Invariant compliance\n   162\t\n   163\tNo callback: a transform is called downward by the caller in the caller's\n   164\tcontrol flow and returns a value; nothing is registered to be invoked\n   165\tlater. No implicit control flow: the four prohibitions. No hidden global\n   166\tstate: keys, policies and decision media are arguments to `wrap`. No shared\n   167\tmutable state beyond what any handle holds. Interpretation and execution\n   168\tstay separate: `apply-request` judges no value. No assumed graph: a chain is\n   169\tan explicit vector.\n   503\t## 6. Lease integration\n   504\t\n   505\tA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\n   506\tgoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\n   507\tpossesses the resource, as that contract requires. What a lease governs:\n   508\t\n   509\t- A table entry a peer serves by its own policy: no lease; the peer retires\n   510\t  it when it likes.\n   511\t- A table entry served for a remote party (a stream a migrated continuation\n   512\t  still reads): yes; the remote party holds it, the holding peer grants and\n   513\t  judges, the identity is the subject.\n   514\t- A relay pair on a meeting peer: yes; the requesting peer holds it, the\n   515\t  meeting peer grants and judges, the two identities are the subject.\n   516\t- Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets: no; governed by close\n   517\t  or by a bounded buffer.\n   518\t- NAT mappings and punch state: no; possessed by the NAT box, so no judge\n   519\t  can exist, and keep-alive cadence is the peer's own (section 4).\n   520\t- Board postings: no; records under the board's retention, each carrying the\n   521\t  lease id of the registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a\n   522\t  posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.\n   523\t\n   524\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   525\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   526\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   527\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   528\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   529\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   530\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   531\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   532\tholder by the medium, the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` names as\n   533\tper-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The renewal\n   534\tmedium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes the\n   535\tsubject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and reports\n   536\tsuccess. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n   537\t\n   538\tUntil ShiBi exists, a renewal medium is reachable by anyone who learns its\n   539\tdescriptor and accepts a third party's renewal, extending a lease it does\n   540\tnot hold: attribution needs no credential, extension may want one, and a\n   541\tcomposition that must not accept it composes a gate on the renewal entry.\n   542\t\n   543\t**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n   544\t:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\n   545\treflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\n   546\trenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\n   547\twriter. A holder with no event writer stops at its bound early, never late.\n   548\t\n   549\t**Reclaim observed.** The mirror answers the `not-found` protocol error for\n   550\ta reclaimed identity and the reflection marks itself gone (2.4). The holder\n   551\tmay also read the grantor's own `:lapsed` record through its reflection on\n   552\t`lease-grants`: that is observing the grantor's stream, not carriage. And\n   553\tthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n   554\t\n   555\t**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers or\n   556\tthe channel's `end` (2.4); the table entry persists, so a holder that\n   557\treconnects within duration plus tolerance finds its entry, its cursors and\n   558\tits lease intact. Expiry is a present answer, the `not-found` error, on a\n   559\thealthy channel. Only expiry frees anything. A meeting peer that restarts\n   560\tloses its table and ledger, so every pair is gone, holders observe\n   561\t`not-found` and re-propose; this is `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant\n   562\twith an empty inventory, correct because the table is process-scoped.\n   563\t\n   564\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`.\n   565\t\n   565\t\n   566\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   567\t\n   568\tThe mechanism is `dao.stream.middleware.md`: a middleware is a map of two\n   569\tpure transforms over the operation map and the outcome map; `wrap` turns a\n   570\thandle and a chain into a handle on the same logical stream; a middleware\n   571\tmaps position `p` of the inner to position `p` of the outer and never\n   572\ttouches a cursor, an anchor, an identity, an id or an outcome kind. This\n   573\tdocument adds the network attachment points: the **table entry** (the\n   574\tserved handle, before it enters the table; the mirror sees only the wrapped\n   575\thandle; gating, value encryption for serving, redaction, metering), the\n   576\t**reflection** (inside the host-composed attach closure; presenting a\n   577\tcredential, value decryption, metering), and the **channel** (reserved for\n   578\ta channel-level wrapper, deferred below).\n   579\t\n   580\tKeys and chains are composition data handed to `wrap`, never descriptor\n   581\tfields. Value-level encryption hides values and leaves identity, ops,\n   582\tcursors, sizes and timing visible. **Channel-level encryption is deferred:\n   583\tthis version specifies no wrapper at the channel attachment point**, its\n   584\tinterface and its ordering against codec and fragmentation (encode, then\n   585\tencrypt, then fragment) being requirements on that future design, not a\n   586\tdesign here. The exact consequence: a relay peer that carries requests and\n   587\tanswers sees every envelope in the clear, identities, ops, cursors, ids,\n   588\tsizes and timing included, and any part of a value not hidden by composed\n   589\tvalue-level middleware. Key distribution is a convention and is not\n   590\tspecified here. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   591\t\n   592\t**The seam.** Authentication and authorization belong to ShiBi\n   593\t(`dao.shibi.md`), unspecified. This document offers only the slot, a\n   594\treserved open key on the request map `:dao.stream.remote/credential` whose\n   595\tvalue is opaque here and travels per request (a per-channel credential is a\n   596\tconvention: the reflection's `present` middleware attaches the same value\n   597\tto every request); the context, `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <attachment\n   598\tidentity>}` handed by the mirror to `apply-request` while local callers\n   599\thand `{}` and no other context key exists in this version; the gate,\n   600\tmirror-side `dao.stream.middleware/gate`, whose `verify` is a pure function\n   601\tof the latest decision, context and request (the decisions are published to\n   602\ta capacity-1 medium by an index interpreter the composition runs over its\n   603\tfact streams) and whose refusal is the contract's `:dao.stream/refused`\n   604\twith an opaque `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`; and the presenter,\n   605\treflection-side `dao.stream.middleware/present`, a pure function of context\n   606\tand request that fills the slot.\n   607\t\n   608\tA ShiBi built as index and query interpreters over a tuple space plugs in\n   609\twithout changing this document: its index interpreter is the interpreter\n   610\tthat folds the streams it observes and publishes decisions onto the\n   611\tdecision medium, its query interpreter is the gate's `verify`, its tokens\n   612\tare the slot's values, and its decisions, where it meters, are the gate's\n   613\tdecision emissions onto a stream the index also observes. Limitation,\n   614\tstated plainly: the seam carries the index's latest decision per medium,\n   615\tnot the history it folded; a query that must reason over history folds it\n   616\tin the index and publishes a derived decision. Until ShiBi exists, gating\n   617\tis an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel` or a pre-shared\n   618\tvalue in the slot.\n   619\t\n   620\tLease attribution (section 6) rests on per-author media alone and needs no\n   621\tcredential; a gate on a renewal medium is an addition.\n   622\t\n   623\t## 8. Unification\n   624\t\n   625\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree, and its fate:\n   626\t\n   627\t- `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart adapters: **subsumed**.\n   628\t  Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium plus the 3.1 projection. Its\n   629\t  accept and disclaim frames and served-path table become unnecessary once\n   630\t  `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is a slice.\n   630\t  `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is a slice.\n   631\t- `dao.stream.serving`: **retired**. Forwards a source into every accepted\n   632\t  socket and flattens a source `gap` into a detach: the copy model. Its\n   633\t  consumers are `yin.repl.serve` and `dao.jing.remote` (which requires and\n   634\t  drives it); both are retired by the slices that follow.\n   635\t- `dao.stream.forward`: **unrelated**. A local copy step; replication by\n   636\t  convention, not exposure.\n   637\t- `dao.stream.rpc`: **convention-over**. Correlation by id over two streams\n   638\t  is section 5's service; its state machine may be kept as one such\n   639\t  vocabulary over reflections.\n   640\t- `dao.stream.rpc.ws`: **retired**. Its only job is translating `:ws/`\n   641\t  envelopes for rpc; the 3.1 projection is the channel binding now.\n   642\t- `dao.stream.apply` as a wire envelope: **retired**. Its ok-or-error\n   643\t  wrapper duplicates the outcome map. The `:dao.stream.apply/call` AST node\n   644\t  and the VM's FFI bridge keep the name and are unrelated to the network.\n   645\t- `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter`, the `daostream:ws://`\n   646\t  URL: **convention-over**. An eval service: `requests` and `answers`\n   647\t  streams, `:op/eval` as payload; the URL becomes a remote descriptor for\n   648\t  that pair. The shared-shell privilege is its interpreter's policy.\n   649\t- `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async`, the `:dao.jing/remote`\n   650\t  coordinate in `dao.jing.coordinate`: **retired**. Deprecated whole;\n   651\t  replaced by `dao.jing.content`, a request and response service (section\n   652\t  5) whose vocabulary, stepped client, driver, async facade and\n   653\t  two-descriptor coordinate are specified in the implementation plan.\n   654\t- `yin.repl.link`, `yin.vm.linker` M3 and M4: **convention-over**. The\n   655\t  content pair is a request and response service over reflections or local\n   656\t  ring buffers; the linker's stepped core is unchanged.\n   657\t- `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`:\n   658\t  **convention-over**. Consume the content client of the implementation\n   659\t  plan; the callback facade is host policy, per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n   660\t- `dao.jing.dht.node` UDP DHT: **unrelated**. Raw JVM datagram RPC with its\n   661\t  own Kademlia envelope; its socket rules and 1200-byte budget are the UDP\n   662\t  channel's template.\n   663\t- `dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`: **subsumed**. The channel\n   664\t  codecs, unchanged.\n   665\t\n   666\t## 9. Contract amendments\n   667\t\n   668\tIn `dao.stream.md`, five amendments and nothing network: OD-1 accepted with\n   669\tthe write fallback \"effect unknown; no automatic retry\" and the\n   670\t`:dao.stream/retry?` key; OD-2 accepted; OD-3 decision 2 accepted and (a)'s\n   671\tendpoint sentence in Envelopes; one `:dao.stream/refused` row on `cursor`,\n   672\t`next` and `append!`; one sentence in Surfaces on a handle composed over\n   673\tanother handle. These five are the whole of section 1's claim on the\n   674\tcontract. The companion edits the unification requires are inventoried in\n   675\tthe implementation plan.\n     1\t# ShiBi\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: stub. ShiBi is not specified and not implemented\n     4\t(`docs/bootstrap.md`, Capability Engine). This page records only what other\n     5\tdocuments may rely on and what ShiBi must supply. It designs nothing.\n     6\t\n     7\t## What it is\n     8\t\n     9\tShiBi is the capability system of datom.world: authentication and\n    10\tauthorization are its job and no other document's. It is to be built the\n    11\tway `dao.space` is built, as a tuple space that emerges from two\n    12\tinterpreters over ordinary streams, one that indexes and one that queries,\n    13\tneither knowing the other exists (`datom.world.md`, Streams). Its tokens are\n    14\tdescribed elsewhere as Macaroon-style: attenuatable, offline-verifiable,\n    15\trevocable caveats (`dao.space.security.md`, Capabilities Govern\n    16\tInterpreters).\n    17\t\n    18\t## How it plugs in\n    19\t\n    20\tShiBi integrates through the middleware seam of\n    21\t[`dao.stream.middleware.md`](./dao.stream.middleware.md) and the attachment\n    22\tpoints of [`dao.stream.remote.md`](./dao.stream.remote.md) section 7, which\n    23\tare fixed and which ShiBi must not change. To plug in it supplies:\n    24\t\n    25\t- a `verify`, a pure function of the latest decision, context and request\n    26\t  that answers nil or a reason: its query interpreter;\n    27\t- an index interpreter over its fact streams, folding with its own cursors\n    28\t  and publishing decisions onto a decision medium a gate reads\n    29\t  (`dao.stream.middleware.md`, gate);\n    30\t- a `present`, a pure function of context and request that produces the\n    31\t  value carried in `:dao.stream.remote/credential`;\n    32\t- a reason vocabulary under its own namespace for\n    33\t  `:dao.stream.middleware/reason`;\n    34\t- where use is metered, the decision facts it emits and indexes.\n    35\t\n    36\tThe contract's refusal outcome is `:dao.stream/refused` and is\n    37\tcapability-free (`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention). Lease attribution\n    38\trests on per-author media and does not depend on ShiBi\n    39\t(`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 6; `yin.repl.link-policy.md`).\n    40\t\n    41\t## What blocks it being load-bearing\n    42\t\n    43\t`dao.stream.discovery.md`, The ShiBi precondition: ShiBi is a capability\n    44\ttoken in one place and a fungible currency in another, and a currency needs\n    45\tdouble-spend resolution, which is global consensus. Until that decision is\n    46\tmade, no design may depend on ShiBi for anything beyond the seam above, and\n    47\tgating in this repository is an allow-list or a pre-shared value.\n   270\t\n   271\t- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal\n   272\t  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\n   273\t  dead holder buys a fresh duration, and continuity is lost when none is\n   274\t  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\n   275\t  tolerance. Partition length alone decides neither.\n   276\t- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\n   277\t- **A medium that gaps more often than one duration** never completes an\n   278\t  observation window and leaks continuously while every component reports\n   279\t  healthy; per-attachment media are the isolation.\n   280\t- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the\n   281\t  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\n   282\t\n   283\t## Carriage\n   284\t\n   285\tProposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\n   286\tpayload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\n   287\tis delivery, not a second authoring.\n   288\t\n   289\t`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\n   290\tremote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\n   291\tordinary `:ws/closed`; for a stream served under `dao.stream.remote.md`, the\n   292\t`not-found` protocol error its mirror answers for the reclaimed identity. A remote holder that\n   293\treads the grantor's stream through a served handle is observing the grantor's\n   294\trecord, not receiving carriage. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing\n   295\treclaim on the wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n   296\t\n   297\t## Out of scope\n   298\t\n   299\t- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\n   300\t- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\n   301\t- **Who may be granted anything at all**, and **what is leasable** — this\n   302\t  contract gates nothing, and the domain decides. `dao.stream.remote.md`\n   303\t  names two subjects: a stream entry served for a remote party, and a relay\n   304\t  pair on a meeting peer.\n    56\t## 2. Completion criteria and implementation slices\n    57\t\n    58\tOrdered. Each names files, host lanes and its proof. Verification commands\n    59\tare in `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`.\n    60\t\n    61\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    62\t| #  | Slice and files                                            | Proves                                                       | Lanes         |\n    63\t+====+============================================================+==============================================================+===============+\n    64\t| 0  | Contract and source alignment: the `dao.stream.md`         | Existing consumers pass; `dao.stream.observe/step` and the   | clj cljs cljd |\n    65\t|    | amendments of the spec's section 9 are accepted;           | engine treat an unrecognized outcome as refused, not a       |               |\n    66\t|    | `:dao.stream/refused` added to the closed outcome sets     | throw; the outcome sets admit the refusal.                   |               |\n    67\t|    | for `cursor`, `next` and `append!` in                      |                                                              |               |\n    68\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc`; `dao.stream.observe`,          |                                                              |               |\n    69\t|    | `yin.vm.engine` follow the unrecognized-outcome rule.      |                                                              |               |\n    70\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    71\t| 1  | `dao.stream.middleware`: `apply-request`, `wrap`, `gate`,  | Position rule under a value cipher over a ring buffer; a     | clj cljs cljd |\n    72\t|    | `present`, a metering exemplar, an index-interpreter        | gate with the channel allow-list refuses with                |               |\n    73\t|    | exemplar publishing decisions. `src/cljc/dao/stream/        | `:dao.stream/refused`; a gate reads the latest decision      |               |\n    74\t|    | middleware.cljc`, tests.                                   | through a capacity-1 medium after eviction; a filter cannot  |               |\n    75\t|    |                                                            | be expressed.                                                |               |\n    76\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    77\t| 2  | `dao.stream.remote` core: `mirror-step`, link, reflection, | The toy over two in-process ring buffers as the channel;     | clj cljs cljd |\n    78\t|    | remote descriptor dispatch, the protocol errors.           | `gap` from an evicting source crosses verbatim with the      |               |\n    79\t|    | `src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc`, tests.                  | source's cursor; `blocked` then `ok` on re-ask; `not-found`  |               |\n    80\t|    |                                                            | marks gone; `no-surface` does not; the descriptor answer     |               |\n    81\t|    |                                                            | carries the surface; a kept cursor from a reflection is      |               |\n    82\t|    |                                                            | accepted by the source handle.                               |               |\n    83\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    84\t| 3  | WebSocket channel composition: `ws-project` at each end;   | The toy across a socket on clj to Node, Node to clj, cljd    | clj cljs cljd |\n    85\t|    | mirror steps over accepted and dialed attachments;         | to clj; both directions serve on one channel; browser        |               |\n    86\t|    | retire `:ws/accept`, disclaim and the served-path table    | dials and reads; a closed connection is observed as the      |               |\n    87\t|    | in `dao.stream.ws`.                                        | link's `end`.                                                |               |\n    88\t+----+------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------+---------------+\n    89\t| 4  | Content service: the `dao.jing.content` family (section 1) | `dao.jing.remote` tests ported and passing over the new      | clj cljs cljd |\n    90\t|    | with `serve-step`, `step`, `driver` (clj), `async`;         | module; `dao.space.index`, btree hydration, the linker M3    |               |\n    91\t|    | `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`; `dao.jing.coordinate` new  